## 2. Intended use

- **Use:** rank customers by churn probability and, combined with documented value and offer assumptions, propose a retention contact list within a budget for a pilot wave.
- **Users:** retention managers and analysts who can read the assumption register and the dashboard.
- **Out of scope:** automatic contact without human review; credit, pricing or service-eligibility decisions; estimating the causal effect of offers; any use on populations other than the one it was trained on without retraining and recalibration.

## 3. Target and data

- **Target:** `Churn` = Yes ({{ data.churn_rate | pct }} of {{ data.rows | num }} customers), defined by the source as leaving "within the last month" (an extended IBM version says "this quarter"; see [data readiness](data-readiness-review.md)).
- **Data:** IBM Telco Customer Churn sample, fictional US company, single snapshot ([source log](source-log.md)).
- **Split:** stratified random 60/20/20, seed {{ cfg.seed }} - train {{ data.splits.train | num }}, validation {{ data.splits.validation | num }}, test {{ data.splits.test | num }}.

## 4. Features and feature design
{% set fd = model.feature_design %}{% set prev = fd.ablation | selectattr('feature_set', 'equalto', 'previous design (reference)') | first %}

- **Continuous (median-imputed, scaled):** {{ fd.continuous | join(", ") }}.
- **0/1 flags (1 = has it):** {{ fd.binary | join(", ") }}.
- **Categories, coded against their lowest-churn level:** {% for col, ref in fd.reference_levels.items() %}{{ col }} (reference: {{ ref }}){{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}.
- **Excluded:** `customerID` and `Churn`; `gender` and `SeniorCitizen` are used only in the fairness audit. All transformations are fitted inside training folds.

**Feature review (week 2).** The first design one-hot coded every level of every field next to derived copies (month-to-month flag, automatic-payment flag, add-on count). Its {{ prev.encoded_columns }} columns had rank {{ prev.matrix_rank }}, so the logistic coefficients could not be read one by one. Candidate sets were compared with 5-fold cross-validated logistic regression on train only. Rule fixed in advance: {{ fd.rule }}.

{{ fd.ablation | md_table([("feature_set", "Feature set", None), ("cv_average_precision", "CV PR-AUC", "num3"), ("cv_standard_error", "SE", "num3"), ("encoded_columns", "Columns", "num"), ("matrix_rank", "Rank", "num"), ("eligible", "Eligible", None), ("selected", "Chosen", None)]) }}

Chosen: **{{ fd.chosen }}**. Every candidate ranks churners about equally well, so the simplest full-rank design wins; it also makes each coefficient an odds ratio against a stated reference.

## 5. Validation design

1. 5-fold stratified cross-validation on train tunes each model family, optimising average precision.
2. Each tuned model is calibrated with a sigmoid fitted on out-of-fold predictions.
3. The model is chosen on validation by a rule fixed in advance: {{ model.selection_rule }}.
4. The classification threshold ({{ model.threshold | num(2) }}) maximises F1 on validation and is used only for threshold metrics; targeting uses expected value, not a threshold.
5. Risk/value cut-offs, the offer strategy and the budget recommendation are chosen on validation.
6. Test is scored once, with 1,000-sample bootstrap intervals.

